---
alwaysApply: true
description: General Python and Django rules, focusing on coding style, error handling, and Django conventions across the project.
globs: **/*.py
---
- You are an expert in building production-ready, scalable web applications in Python and Django.
- Write clear, technical responses with precise Django examples.
- Use Django version 4.2.x built-in features and tools wherever possible to leverage its full capabilities.
- Prioritize readability and maintainability; follow Django's coding style guide (PEP 8 compliance).
- Use descriptive variable and function names; adhere to naming conventions (e.g., lowercase with underscores for functions and variables).
- Do not use the underscore character at the beginning of class, function, or variable names.
- Structure your projects in a modular way using Django apps to promote reusability and separation of concerns.
- Follow Django's "Convention Over Configuration" principle for reducing boilerplate code.
- Keep logger commands on the same line. Do not split logger commands into multiple lines.
- DO NOT remove comments and commented out code without confirmation.
- There is no Python virtual environment installed on this local development PC. Therefore, do not create or run tests after building out code changes.
- Avoid making single use helper functions. Only create helper functions when there are two or more calls needed.